::SPEECH_U01_L01_GP_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
During a group discussion, one student says, "Our opening needs to explain the topic before we give examples." Which response best shows active listening?
{
~A. That is obvious, so we should move on.#This dismisses the speaker instead of showing understanding. Active listening should show that the listener heard the idea and is willing to work with it.
=B. You think the audience needs the main idea first. What should we explain in the opening?#Correct. This paraphrases the idea and asks a useful follow-up question, which supports collaborative communication.
~C. I already wrote my part, so I do not want to change anything.#This focuses only on the listener's own work. Collaboration requires responding to the group's shared goal.
~D. We should ignore examples completely.#This changes the meaning of the speaker's idea. The speaker wanted better order, not removal of examples.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_GP_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which action is the clearest example of collaboration during speech planning?
{
~A. Choosing the topic alone and telling the group later.#This leaves out the group and does not support a shared decision. Collaboration requires participation from others.
~B. Waiting silently while others do all the planning.#Silence alone is not collaboration. A group member should listen and contribute when appropriate.
~C. Correcting every person immediately before they finish speaking.#Interrupting blocks listening and can shut down ideas. A collaborative speaker allows ideas to be completed.
=D. Asking each group member for ideas and helping combine them into one plan.#Correct. This includes others, listens to ideas, and helps the group move toward a shared plan.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_GP_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
A listener says, "So you are saying our speech needs more practice before we record it." What active listening skill is the listener using?
{
=A. Paraphrasing the speaker's idea#Correct. The listener restates the idea in their own words to check understanding before responding.
~B. Changing the subject#The listener stays with the speaker's idea. Changing the subject would move away from the message.
~C. Attacking the speaker#The listener does not insult or blame the speaker. The response focuses on understanding the idea.
~D. Ending the discussion#The listener is keeping the discussion open by checking meaning.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_GP_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.11
Question:
In an online class discussion, a student is confused about the task. Which response best supports social and instructional communication?
{
~A. You should already know what to do.#This response is dismissive and does not help the student understand the task.
~B. I am finished, so this is not my problem.#This ignores the communication need. Social and instructional communication should help clarify school tasks.
=C. The task asks us to give one idea and one reason. I can show where the directions say that.#Correct. This response explains the task and points to the directions, which supports understanding.
~D. Just write anything quickly.#This may lead to incomplete work. Helpful communication should clarify expectations, not encourage guessing.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L01_GP_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L01_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which response best uses academic communication during a group speech discussion?
{
~A. Your idea is bad.#This is too vague and personal. Academic communication should focus on ideas and reasons.
=B. I understand your point, but I think the evidence should come before the example because the audience needs context.#Correct. This response uses clear academic language, gives a reason, and keeps the focus on the speech.
~C. Whatever, do it your way.#This avoids communication instead of helping the group make a reasoned decision.
~D. I do not care about the audience.#This ignores the purpose of communication. Speakers should consider audience understanding.
}
